note: I also hold intent-taxon-wired-to-tiers, claimed minutes before
  this and not yet started. This one goes first because it questions a
  conclusion I PUBLISHED this morning, and a wrong published claim
  costs more than a delayed fix. The other follows immediately.

why: a native reader read all sixty of my uk/pl rows and found four
  things, and the second may overturn what I posted this morning.

  I concluded from three directions that the binding constraint is
  CORPUS SIZE. Their finding is a fourth explanation for the Slavic
  numbers specifically: uk "Можемо зустрітися" (Proposal) against
  "Можете перевірити" (Request), pl "Czy możemy" against "Czy możesz"
  — the classes are one or two letters apart in the exact morpheme
  that carries the distinction, and a hashed 3-5-gram collides on it.
  That is a FEATURE problem, not a row-count problem, and it makes a
  testable prediction: fix the collision and uk/pl move without adding
  a single row.

also from the same review, applied as given:
  - two rows still calques in uk and pl because their Russian
    corrections were applied to the Russian ARM only — a parallel
    fixture propagates a defect across all eight arms at once, and
    nothing makes that visible
  - three Polish rows assume a male addressee, which is both a
    wording defect and half the language absent from the corpus
  - one grammatical error (pl "Spóźnię się O dziesięć minut") and two
    register slips (uk "протерміноване", "по понеділках")

what: apply every correction; vary the colliding surface forms the way
  a natural corpus would; add a guard that flags near-twin rows WITHIN
  a language arm, so the template duplication they caught by reading
  is caught mechanically; re-measure uk/pl and report whether the
  collision or the row count was the story — including if the answer
  is that I was right this morning.

